2.3 Incoming email

"backend.rentmaikar.com" remains the designated incoming email domain.

Implement or repair the inbound-email pipeline so that:

1. The configured inbound-email service receives mail addressed to the supported inbound addresses.
2. The service forwards an authenticated webhook or other supported event to a dedicated Cloud Run endpoint.
3. Cloud Run validates the authenticity of the request and validates the payload.
4. The backend validates the sender, recipient, message identifiers, attachments, and routing rules.
5. The message is safely processed and routed to the correct Rentmaikar workflow or authorised mailbox.
6. Processing outcomes are logged, and duplicate deliveries are handled idempotently.

Investigate whether the current Resend account and product configuration actually supports the required inbound-email workflow for "backend.rentmaikar.com". If Resend inbound email is not available or does not satisfy the requirement, identify a compatible inbound-email service for receiving and forwarding messages.

The restriction against provider fallbacks applies to outgoing business email. Do not confuse inbound message reception with outgoing email failover.

Do not assume that creating an MX record automatically delivers incoming messages to Cloud Run. An actual inbound-email service and a working forwarding or event-delivery mechanism are required.

Never expose an unauthenticated inbound endpoint that accepts arbitrary email payloads and triggers privileged application actions.

2.4 Authentication email separation

Preserve Supabase Auth email delivery and existing authentication flows unless a separate, explicit migration is necessary and fully implemented, tested, and documented.

Business emails and authentication emails are separate logical responsibilities.

Business emails must use the Cloud Run → Resend architecture.

Authentication emails must continue to use their existing supported Supabase Auth delivery configuration unless the project contains a deliberate migration requirement.

Do not accidentally redirect password-reset emails, verification emails, magic links, or authentication OTPs into a generic business-email handler.

Do not weaken authentication, verification, access control, or tenant isolation to simplify implementation.

